00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.480
The top story centers on a developer who built an open-source CUDA kernel that performs top-k

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:14.920
vector search entirely on GPU. This eliminates the PCIe transfer latency that previously forced

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.220
retrieval results to bounce off the CPU during agentic rag inference.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:25.800
The approach replaces standard CPU mediated vector search with a device resident kernel.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:32.720
It produces deterministic microsecond scale tail latencies instead of variable round trip costs.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:40.080
This directly addresses the silent bottleneck in long horizon agents where retrieval happens repeatedly

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.520
inside tool loops. The change preserves answer quality while making tail latency predictable.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:52.800
That matters for production agent deployments that must meet strict response time SLAs.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.880
Teams building retrieval augmented agents should benchmark the kernel against their

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:03.880
